# Production Migration Guide: Cloudinary Signed Uploads

This document outlines how to move from current **Unsigned** testing to **Signed** production uploads.

## Why Switch?
Unsigned uploads allow anyone with your `cloud_name` and `upload_preset` to upload images to your Cloudinary account. Signed uploads require a server-side signature, ensuring only your application can upload.

## Final Implementation Prompt
Copy and paste this prompt when you are ready to transition:

> "Please upgrade my Cloudinary implementation to **Signed Uploads**. 
> 1. Create a server-side signature endpoint in `app/api/upload/sign/route.ts` using the `cloudinary` SDK and `CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET`.
> 2. Update `lib/cloudinary-upload.ts` to request a timestamp and signature from that endpoint before performing the `fetch` to Cloudinary.
> 3. Add `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_API_KEY` to the upload payload.
> 4. Ensure the CSP in `next.config.ts` handles the new flow."

## Implementation Checklist

### 1. Cloudinary Dashboard
- [ ] Create a new **Signed** upload preset.
- [ ] Set "Return delete token" to off (unless needed).
- [ ] Restrict allowed formats to `png, jpg, svg, webp`.

### 2. Environment Variables
Add these to your production `.env`:
- `CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET` (Keep this secret!)
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_API_KEY` (Publicly safe)
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOUDINARY_UPLOAD_PRESET` (Set to your signed preset name)

### 3. Files to Update
- `lib/cloudinary-upload.ts`: Add signature fetching logic.
- `app/api/upload/sign/route.ts`: New file for backend signing.
- `next.config.ts`: Ensure `connect-src` and `img-src` are still valid.
